479
00:31:15,060 --> 00:31:19,320
separate organ system or
independent mechanical

480
00:31:19,320 --> 00:31:23,800
system, then it's not moved
by voluntary movement.

481
00:31:23,860 --> 00:31:28,720
It's moved by involuntary
action. And so that really,

482
00:31:28,720 --> 00:31:32,900
in many ways, in my mind,
came from the development of

483
00:31:32,900 --> 00:31:36,320
the cranial concept, where
one of the things Dr.

484
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:40,540
Sutherland says early on is,
you know as soon as I realized

485
00:31:40,540 --> 00:31:44,740
that motion was present I
looked for the muscles that

486
00:31:44,740 --> 00:31:48,260
crossed the joints and you'll
see muscles that attach

487
00:31:48,260 --> 00:31:51,800
to the head and go elsewhere
but no muscles that go

488
00:31:51,800 --> 00:31:55,940
from one bone to the other to
move it like the rotatories

489
00:31:56,140 --> 00:32:00,140
and inner transversary I do
for intersegmental motion

490
00:32:00,140 --> 00:32:04,360
in the spine and so since
there was no possibility of

491
00:32:04,360 --> 00:32:07,900
voluntary movement that
brought him into the realm of

492
00:32:07,900 --> 00:32:11,720
involuntary action, which
I think is very much a part

493
00:32:11,720 --> 00:32:14,460
of the discovery of the
primary respiratory mechanism

494
00:32:14,460 --> 00:32:21,320
and involuntary physiological
movement in the cranium.

495
00:32:22,220 --> 00:32:26,380
So as we look at the
membranes and their role

496
00:32:26,380 --> 00:32:29,980
then, they would be
to guide and limit the

497
00:32:29,980 --> 00:32:33,320
motion of the cranial
bones. And to do so, then

498
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:36,620
they will need to
attach to each of the

499
00:32:36,620 --> 00:32:38,780
cranial bones. We'll look
at those attachments.

500
00:32:39,120 --> 00:32:43,100
And ultimately, when you
examine babies and little

501
00:32:43,100 --> 00:32:49,020
ones, remember that the
bones are soft and there's no

502
00:32:49,020 --> 00:32:53,420
articulations between them.
And so then the membranes

503
00:32:53,420 --> 00:32:58,640
really serve a functional
role of stability, maintaining

504
00:32:58,640 --> 00:33:02,050
shape and stability. And
a lot of the mechanical

505
00:33:02,060 --> 00:33:08,100
problems in babies are membranous
in their origin. So when

506
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:13,400
he was talking about how
the membranes or ligaments

507
00:33:15,220 --> 00:33:18,600
guide and limit motion,
he introduced the concept

508
00:33:18,600 --> 00:33:23,320
of reciprocal tension. So
there's a couple of simple

509
00:33:23,320 --> 00:33:26,940
ways. And again, the
concepts are the same,

510
00:33:26,940 --> 00:33:31,020
whether you're in the head
or in the body, it just gets

511
00:33:31,020 --> 00:33:34,180
a little more complex
in the head. And so here

512
00:33:34,180 --> 00:33:39,460
we have a reciprocal tension
membrane in the forearm.

513
00:33:39,490 --> 00:33:42,580
So here you can see
fibers in this direction,

514
00:33:42,580 --> 00:33:45,360
here you can see fibers
in this direction. And so

515
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:49,320
reciprocal tension means
that I've got motion in

516
00:33:49,320 --> 00:33:52,500
different directions, and
I've got fibers oriented in

517
00:33:52,500 --> 00:33:56,980
those directions to help
limit. And so this is

518
00:33:56,980 --> 00:34:00,120
something that you see in
a lot of different places.

519
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:03,880
Here's another reciprocal
tension ligament system

520
00:34:03,880 --> 00:34:08,040
now. This is the
clavicle coming into the

521
00:34:08,040 --> 00:34:11,920
manubrium and first rib.
And you see the ligaments,

522
00:34:12,100 --> 00:34:14,300
you know, they just look
like a big bundle of

523
00:34:14,300 --> 00:34:18,020
ligaments. When you dig in,
you can see this direction

524
00:34:18,020 --> 00:34:20,740
and you can see this
direction perpendicular,

525
00:34:20,980 --> 00:34:26,820
able to then directly
limit movement in opposing

526
00:34:26,820 --> 00:34:29,540
directions because
they're perpendicular to

527
00:34:29,540 --> 00:34:32,720
one another at that
sternoclavicular joint. So

528
00:34:32,720 --> 00:34:35,860
that would be a reciprocal
tension ligament.

529
00:34:36,720 --> 00:34:41,240
When we get to the cranium
then, now we've got a

530
00:34:41,240 --> 00:34:45,120
modified sphere. And so
now instead of kind of

531
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:48,240
two-plane motion, we've
got three-plane motion.

532
00:34:48,240 --> 00:34:51,640
And so those perpendicular
fibers now have to be in

533
00:34:51,640 --> 00:34:56,560
three planes. So in the
AP plane or sagittal

534
00:34:56,560 --> 00:35:00,940
plane, we have the falc
cerebri, and in the transverse

535
00:35:00,940 --> 00:35:04,700
plane, the tentorium
cerebelli. And that was

536
00:35:04,700 --> 00:35:07,420
really what Dr. Sutherland
described as the

537
00:35:07,420 --> 00:35:11,080
reciprocal tension membrane,
the falcs and the tent.

538
00:35:12,880 --> 00:35:18,320
Again, Dr. Sutherland was
a very logical thinker.

539
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:21,320
So if I'm going to
specifically guide and

540
00:35:21,320 --> 00:35:24,260
limit motion of the bones
in relation to one another,

541
00:35:24,690 --> 00:35:27,340
I need an attachment
to every one of them.

542
00:35:27,340 --> 00:35:31,040
So starting in the front
here, we have attachment

543
00:35:31,040 --> 00:35:34,080
to the ethmoid,
attachment to the frontal,

544
00:35:34,140 --> 00:35:37,980
