coming to the back.

545
00:35:38,470 --> 00:35:41,060
Here, I'm hoping you can
see my arrow attachment

546
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:44,920
to the occiput, and then
here is where it adjoins

547
00:35:44,920 --> 00:35:48,960
the tent, attaching
again to occiput, coming

548
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:51,700
around to temporal. This
is the petrous temporal,

549
00:35:51,700 --> 00:35:55,360
and then finally ending
up on the sphenoid.

550
00:35:55,780 --> 00:36:01,680
And so another concept
that he included was the

551
00:36:01,680 --> 00:36:05,780
idea that if the membranes
are going to move the

552
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:11,960
bones, they need power.
And the power in movement

553
00:36:11,960 --> 00:36:16,380
comes from leverage or a
lever, and that lever has to

554
00:36:16,380 --> 00:36:20,160
have a fulcrum. And so
he described a fulcrum

555
00:36:20,160 --> 00:36:24,360
within this primary respiratory
mechanism as somewhere

556
00:36:24,360 --> 00:36:28,240
where the fulks and
tent adjoin one another,

557
00:36:28,240 --> 00:36:30,860
which is, of course, the
area of the straight sinus.

558
00:36:31,300 --> 00:36:37,580
And, you know, fulcrums
in diagnosis and treatment

559
00:36:37,580 --> 00:36:41,320
are important. They're
still points around

560
00:36:41,320 --> 00:36:45,540
which motion occurs. And
if you can identify them,

561
00:36:45,900 --> 00:36:50,140
you can use them and
sometimes make your treatments

562
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:54,400
just much more powerful. So
that's why I included it.

563
00:36:54,640 --> 00:36:58,680
And then finally, we have
what's called the core link,

564
00:36:58,680 --> 00:37:03,800
or the fact that attaching
firmly at foramen magnum

565
00:37:03,800 --> 00:37:07,320
as the lowest point of
cranial dura, that dura

566
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:11,960
continues as the wrapping of
the spinal cord and spinal

567
00:37:11,960 --> 00:37:17,780
dura, which has firm
attachments at C2, C3, and then

568
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:22,320
falls relatively free
until it has, again, a firm

569
00:37:22,320 --> 00:37:29,620
attachment at S2. And so that
sort of connection between

570
00:37:29,620 --> 00:37:34,920
the foramen magnum at
the top and the sacrum at

571
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:39,820
the bottom brings the sacrum
into being part of the

572
00:37:39,820 --> 00:37:43,760
primary respiratory mechanism.
It can't move unless

573
00:37:43,760 --> 00:37:48,300
the sacrum moves between the
ilia. And so, at the very

574
00:37:48,300 --> 00:37:52,280
least, the sacrum is part
of your cranial concept.

575
00:37:52,500 --> 00:37:56,500
And excluding treatment
of the pelvis in someone

576
00:37:56,500 --> 00:37:59,520
who, let's say, has a
bad head injury, and

577
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:02,420
you want to treat the
cranium, would be a

578
00:38:02,420 --> 00:38:05,700
terrible mistake. If the
sacrum is really bound

579
00:38:05,700 --> 00:38:09,620
up, that head is not
going to open up and move.

580
00:38:09,760 --> 00:38:13,540
And so, part of his cranial
concept, whether you

581
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:17,140
want to consider the movement
of the sacrum, just one

582
00:38:17,220 --> 00:38:20,940
of the movement of the
bones or as a separate,

583
00:38:21,770 --> 00:38:25,380
sometimes it was listed
separately, just to sort of

584
00:38:25,380 --> 00:38:29,720
emphasize the sacrum as an
important component. And

585
00:38:29,720 --> 00:38:33,640
then the movement, he
described it like a sickle. A

586
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:37,920
sickle is like the the
scythe, where it's a blade

587
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:42,940
that curves more than 180
degrees. And so as it swings

588
00:38:42,940 --> 00:38:46,940
forward, as you swing that
forward from the back,

589
00:38:46,940 --> 00:38:50,040
the front of it turns on
itself and actually moves

590
00:38:50,040 --> 00:38:53,200
inward. And so you can see
that and how that's going

591
00:38:53,200 --> 00:38:57,540
to sort of guide and limit
that overall change in

592
00:38:57,540 --> 00:39:02,060
AP diameter that occurs
during that inhalation

593
00:39:02,060 --> 00:39:07,640
phase. And the tent also
kind of like a sickle, also

594
00:39:07,640 --> 00:39:11,580
as arcing around in its
attachment to the sphenoid.

595
00:39:12,860 --> 00:39:17,940
And the idea as a separate
component is that you

596
00:39:17,940 --> 00:39:23,980
can have dysfunction that is
primarily in the membranes,

597
00:39:23,980 --> 00:39:26,200
the same way in the body,
you can have fascial

598
00:39:26,200 --> 00:39:32,360
strains. They're longer
strains, strains that are

599
00:39:32,360 --> 00:39:36,720
more than just across a
single joint. They often will

600
00:39:36,720 --> 00:39:41,220
extend a great distance.
And learning to identify

601
00:39:41,220 --> 00:39:44,980
this, remember those
components are all observed,

602
00:39:45,100 --> 00:39:49,140
learning to kind of
distinguish a membranous or

603
00:39:49,140 --> 00:39:53,620
fascial strain from the
articular strains that occur

604
00:39:53,620 --> 00:39:56,600
at either end of that
fascial strain can really

605
00:39:56,600 --> 00:40:00,580
